  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday: Upper-level troughing off the PNW/British Columbia coast \nwill move inland, and the associated shortwave will usher in a dry \ncold front across the Inland Northwest. The resultant dry and gusty \nconditions will lead to elevated to locally critical fire weather \nconcerns to central and eastern Washington. Areas with the most risk \ninclude the Waterville Plateau and Okanogan Valley, which have a 20-\n50% chance of maximum wind gusts 40 mph or greater this afternoon. \nElsewhere, expect wind gusts 20 to 25 mph with sustained winds 10 to \n15 mph. With minimum relative humidity values mostly staying above \n20 percent, fire weather headlines have not been issued for this \nafternoon, but any new or ongoing fires may still experience rapid \nspread due to the combination of dry conditions and gusty winds. \nThere is a 10 to 20 percent chance of very light showers at the \nCascade crest, but elsewhere chances are at or very near zero. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Once the cold front moves through, the \nregion will again be in between offshore troughing and a ridge over \nthe central US. Little change in temperatures will be seen, with \nhighs remaining in the mid to high 80s and lows in the mid 50s. By \nThursday, the ridge will begin strengthening and retrograding back \nwest, centering over the Desert Southwest to end the work week. This \nwill result in an uptick in temperatures with Friday being the \nwarmest day of the week and expected highs in the low 90s. This will \nbring a return of mostly isolated Moderate HeatRisk values, and all \nthose sensitive to heat-related illnesses should take precautions to \nremain adequately cool and hydrated. While winds on Wednesday will \nbe seasonably breezy, the thermal gradient increasing on Thursday \nand Friday will bring wind gusts 15-20 mph to the basin, Cascade \ngaps, Waterville Plateau, and Okanogan Valley areas. These two days \nwill be elevated fire weather days due to the drier air mass ushered \nin by the cold front dropping relative humidity values into the \nteens.\n\nNext weekend: Long-term guidance and cluster analysis continue to \nstrongly support the next potential critical fire weather day being \nSaturday. An upper level trough will usher another cold front \nthrough the area, alonside a strong surface level low on the lee \nside of the Rockies creating a strong pressure gradient with the off \nshore surface high. This will result in a strong push of winds \nalongside continued dry conditions. Looking at NBM probabilities, a \nmajority of the forecast area, there is a 60 percent chance and \nabove at 30 mph maximum wind gusts. When looking at the chance for \n40 mph wind gusts, the chances shrink down to the Waterville \nPlateau, Okaongan Valley, and upper Columbia Basin areas, which have \na 40 to 50 percent chance and and above. Within the Waterville \nPlateau area, there is even a 30% chance and above for wind gusts 50 \nmph or higher. These probabilities support the potential for a \ncritical fire weather day, and conditions will be very closely \nmonitored as any new ignitions or ongoing fires could experience \nrapid experience rapid spread. Throughout the week into next \nweekend, chances for precipitation remain at or near zero percent,\nbringing very little relief at this point of the fire season.\n/AS",
